'\" t
.TH DOT 1 "2026-08-21" "dotfiles" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dot \- manage the dotfiles repository: links, system files, packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dot
.I command
.RI [ profile ]
.PP
.B dot
is not an installed binary. It is a shell alias to
.IR $DOTFILES_DIR/dot ,
defined in
.IR .zshrc .
Run it as
.B ./dot
from the repository if the alias is not loaded.
.PP
.BR Note :
graphviz also ships a
.BR dot (1).
This page shadows it for the current user. Reach the other one with
.BR "man -M /usr/share/man dot" .
.SH DESCRIPTION
One repository holds the whole environment and deploys it in four different ways.
.PP
.TS
tab(@) allbox;
lb lb lb
l lx lb.
Directory@What it holds@Deployed as
modules/@T{
Whatever belongs in $HOME. \fImodules/shell/.zshrc\fR maps to \fI~/.zshrc\fR. Editing the file in $HOME edits the repository.
T}@symlinks
system/<scope>/root/@T{
Whatever belongs outside $HOME. \fIsystem/common/root/etc/x\fR maps to \fI/etc/x\fR. Copied, not linked, because these files are read at boot before /home is necessarily mounted.
T}@copies
packages/<distro>/@T{
Package lists, one file per group.
T}@installed
suckless/@T{
Patched sources of dwm, st, dmenu, slock, dwmblocks. Compiled in place, since \fBpatch\fR refuses to touch a symlink.
T}@compiled
.TE
.PP
A
.B scope
is a reason to deploy a file:
.IR common ,
the distribution
.RI ( arch ,
.IR ubuntu ),
the hardware
.RI ( laptop ,
.IR peripherals ),
the graphical environment
.RI ( x11\-dwm ,
.IR hyprland ).
Splitting them keeps a tower from receiving the touchpad and backlight rules of a
laptop.
.PP
A
.B profile
names which modules, which scopes and which package groups go together. It never
names a distribution, since that is detected from
.IR /etc/os\-release .
The profile comes from
.IR machine.d/<hostname>.conf ,
falling back to the chassis type.
.I common
and the detected distribution are always added.
.PP
Every command takes an optional profile. Without one the detected profile is used,
and
.B dot status
always says which it took and where that came from.
.SH COMMANDS
.TS
tab(@) allbox;
lb lb
lb lx.
Command@What it does
dot link [prof]@T{
Create the symlinks. Backs up anything in the way as \fI.bak-<date>\fR. Idempotent. The repair command for everything \fBdot status\fR flags.
T}
dot unlink [prof]@T{
Remove the links this profile created, and only those. Keeps the backups.
T}
dot list [prof]@T{
Print every path the profile manages, one per line.
T}
dot adopt <path>@T{
Move a file or directory from $HOME into a module and leave a symlink behind.
T}
dot status [prof]@T{
Full drift report, read only, never prompts. \fBdot st\fR is a synonym.
T}
dot audit [what]@T{
What this machine actually uses, as opposed to what is installed. Arch only.
T}
dot state@T{
Regenerate \fIsystem/state.md\fR: enabled units, groups, shell, locale, timezone.
T}
dot system-apply@T{
Copy the profile scopes of \fIsystem/\fR onto the root filesystem. Needs sudo.
T}
dot system-pull@T{
The other direction: copy the system version back into the repository if it differs.
T}
dot install [prof]@T{
Install the package groups of the profile with the detected package manager.
T}
dot build-suckless@T{
Build and install dwm, st, dmenu, slock and dwmblocks from \fIsuckless/\fR. Needs sudo.
T}
dot fonts@T{
Install the fonts that no package provides.
T}
dot nvim-patch@T{
Apply the patches that a plugin's own API cannot express.
T}
dot sync@T{
Regenerate everything generated, then \fBgit add -A\fR. Does not commit.
T}
dot bootstrap [prof]@T{
A bare machine to a full environment, in one chain. Asks before touching anything.
T}
.TE
.PP
The details that matter follow.
.SS Links
.TP
.BI "dot link " [profile]
Anything already sitting at a target path is renamed to
.I <name>.bak\-<date>
before the link is made, so nothing is ever destroyed. Rerun it freely. It refuses
to do anything at all if two modules claim the same path.
.IP
.B Use it
after a
.BR "git pull" ,
after adding a module to a profile, and to repair anything
.B dot status
reports as missing, blocked or broken. Never needed right after
.BR "dot adopt" .
.TP
.BI "dot adopt " path " " [module]
It
.B moves
the file into the module and leaves a symlink behind, so there is nothing to run
afterwards. Given a directory, it adopts each file inside one at a time and leaves
the directory itself real in
.IR $HOME .
.IP
Without
.IR module ,
it asks, showing which profiles each module reaches. A file lives in exactly one
module. It refuses a path that is already a symlink, a path outside
.IR $HOME ,
or a file the module already carries. Nested git repositories and daemon state are
skipped and reported. Remember to commit afterwards.
.SS Outside $HOME
.TP
.B dot system-apply
Preserves each target's mode. Reload afterwards with
.B udevadm control --reload
and
.BR "systemctl daemon-reload" .
.TP
.B dot system-pull
Only touches files the repository already tracks. It never discovers new ones, so
a genuinely new
.I /etc
file must be placed in
.I system/<scope>/root/
by hand once.
.SS Packages and build
.TP
.B dot install
On Arch, if the profile sets
.IR "aur: yes" ,
the AUR list is installed too with
.B paru
or
.BR yay .
Without either, the AUR step is skipped with a warning.
.TP
.B dot build-suckless
For each of the five projects:
.B make clean
then
.B sudo make install
inside
.IR dotfiles/suckless/<project> .
The repository is the build tree, and the artefacts it produces are gitignored.
Finishes by setting the setuid bit on
.IR /usr/local/bin/slock ,
which slock's own Makefile already does. Without that bit slock cannot read
.I /etc/shadow
and refuses to start, so a missing bit cannot lock you out.
.IP
Binaries go to
.IR /usr/local/bin .
A running dwm keeps the old one until the session restarts.
.TP
.B dot nvim-patch
Applies the patches under
.I modules/nvim/.config/nvim/patches/
to the installed lazy.nvim plugins. Already applied patches are detected and
skipped. If upstream moved and a patch no longer applies, it says so rather than
forcing.
.SS Everything at once
.TP
.B dot sync
In order:
.BR "dot system-pull" ,
the Arch AUR list,
.BR "dot state" ,
then
.BR "git add -A" .
It stops there. The commit is yours to write.
.TP
.BI "dot bootstrap " [profile]
Chains
.BR install ", " link ", " system-apply ", " fonts ", " build-suckless " and " nvim-patch ,
unless the profile overrides the chain with
.IR steps: .
Called without an argument it prints what it takes the machine to be and asks for
confirmation, because a tower mistaken for a laptop would receive touchpad,
backlight and lid rules.
.SH READING DOT STATUS
Each section prints a grey legend giving its direction of travel. The sections are
independent: one can be red while the others are green.
.SS links
Compares
.I dotfiles/modules/<module>/<path>
with
.IR ~/<path> .
.PP
.TS
tab(@) allbox;
lb lb lb
lb lx lb.
Verdict@What it means, and why@Fix
linked@T{
The link exists and points at the module.
T}@nothing
missing from ~@T{
The module has the file, $HOME does not. A file reached the repository without passing through you, typically a \fBgit pull\fR, or a module was added to the profile.
T}@dot link
real file, not linked@T{
A real file occupies the path where the link should be, counted as \fIblocked\fR. A tool rewrote it in place rather than through the link (\fBsed -i\fR and several editors replace the inode and destroy the symlink), you created it by hand, or a fresh install dropped its own default there.
T}@dot link
broken link@T{
The link exists, its target does not. The file was renamed or deleted in the repository, or the repository moved.
T}@dot link
link points elsewhere@T{
The link aims at something other than the module, counted as \fIblocked\fR. A leftover from an earlier installation.
T}@dot link
.TE
.PP
In short,
.B dot link
answers everything that is not
.BR linked ,
and it backs up before it replaces. If the version it backed up was the one you
wanted, copy the contents of
.I <name>.bak\-<date>
into the module and rerun.
.PP
A separate error,
.IR "claimed by several modules" ,
means two modules ship the same path.
.B dot link
refuses entirely until one gives it up, usually by moving the shared file into a
module both depend on, such as
.IR x11\-common .
.SS system/ vs the root filesystem
Compares
.I dotfiles/system/<scope>/root/<path>
with
.IR /<path> ,
for the scopes the profile carries.
.PP
.TS
tab(@) allbox;
lb lb lb
lbw(13) lx lbw(17).
Verdict@What it means, and why@Fix
identical@T{
The two copies match.
T}@nothing
diverges@T{
They differ. The report names both paths and says which copy is newer. When both directions appear at once it names them and lets you choose.
T}@T{
see the two rows below
T}
repo is newer@T{
You edited the repository copy and never deployed it.
T}@dot system-apply
system is newer@T{
Something edited the live file, or you did, and the repository has not caught up.
T}@dot system-pull
missing from the system@T{
The repository has the file, the root filesystem does not.
T}@dot system-apply
cannot check without root@T{
The file lives in a directory only root may traverse, such as \fI/etc/polkit-1/rules.d\fR. It is not missing, it is unverifiable. \fBdot status\fR never prompts for a password.
T}@T{
.nf
sudo -v, rerun
.fi
T}
.TE
.SS packages, secrets, git
.TS
tab(@) allbox;
lb lb lb
lb lx lb.
Section@What it checks@Fix
packages@T{
What is installed but absent from \fIdotfiles/packages/<distro>/*.txt\fR. The reverse case, catalogued but not installed, is not an error and is not reported.
T}@dot sync
secrets@T{
Any sensitive file tracked by git: SSH and GPG keys, \fIrclone.conf\fR, \fIatuin/config.toml\fR, \fI.npmrc\fR, \fIgh/hosts.yml\fR, \fIsolaar/config.yaml\fR. A hit is serious.
T}@T{
.nf
git rm --cached
check .gitignore
.fi
T}
git@T{
The working tree of the repository itself, in the same three buckets as \fBgit status\fR, plus how far ahead or behind the upstream is. Uncommitted work is normal and never makes the command fail.
T}@dotgit add, commit
.TE
.SH FILES
.TS
tab(@) allbox;
lb lb
lb lx.
Path@What it is
dot@T{
The CLI. Everything goes through it.
T}
modules/<module>/@T{
What goes into $HOME, by theme.
T}
system/<scope>/root/@T{
What goes outside $HOME, by reason.
T}
packages/<distro>/@T{
Package lists, one file per group.
T}
profiles/<name>.conf@T{
Modules, scopes and package groups that go together.
T}
machine.d/<host>.conf@T{
Which profile this machine defaults to. Not versioned.
T}
suckless/@T{
Patched sources, and the build tree. No binaries are committed.
T}
examples/@T{
Templates for the local files that are never versioned.
T}
system/state.md@T{
Reference state of the machine, generated by \fBdot state\fR.
T}
.TE
.SH EXAMPLES
.TS
tab(@) allbox;
lb lb
lw(30) lx.
Situation@What to run
T{
Editing a file the repository already tracks. The path in $HOME is a link into the repository, so the editor writes straight into it.
T}@T{
.nf
nothing, then
dotgit commit
.fi
T}
T{
A new config file worth keeping.
T}@T{
.nf
dot adopt <path> <module>
dot sync
dotgit commit -m "..."
.fi
T}
T{
Picking up what lives outside $HOME: /etc, the AUR list, the machine state.
T}@T{
.nf
dot sync
dotgit commit -m "..."
.fi
T}
T{
After pulling changes made on another machine.
T}@T{
.nf
dotgit pull
dot link
dot system-apply
.fi
T}
T{
A bare machine, once.
T}@T{
.nf
git clone --recursive <url>
cd dotfiles
\&./dot status
\&./dot bootstrap
.fi
T}
T{
Something looks wrong and you want to know what.
T}@T{
.nf
dot status
man dot
.fi
T}
.TE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DOTFILES_DIR
Repository location, set in
.IR .zshrc .
The
.B dot
and
.B dotgit
aliases are built from it.
.TP
.B DOT_DISTRO
Force the detected distribution instead of reading
.IR /etc/os\-release .
Useful for testing another distribution's lists.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B dot status
exits non zero when something is wrong: a broken or blocked link, a divergence
with the root filesystem, or a sensitive file tracked by git. Uncommitted work
does not count.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR stow (1),
.BR man (1).
.PP
The repository README covers installing on a new machine, the five machine
specific files, and the reasoning behind the design.
